"# I’m looping in Chat GPT on my X-Men pitch\nAnd in doing so, learning about what it can and can’t do.\n(Worth noting, this is GPT 3 I think, and they’re on at least 4 now)\n\nI went through my whole pitch, step by step, and it responded at length for each step so I’m not gonna copy much into here, just the interesting things.\nI gave it the contents of my document I have, and added on my semi-recent idea about burning Kitty’s house down.\n\nI’ll note right off the bat that, for each section I wrote, the AI would restate what I said, compliment it, and then expand on the details a little to emphasized generically important things. Just... yeah, restating what I’m saying with more words and more on the nose things like “This would provide a powerful and emotional conclusion to her personal arc” and “I must say, that twist ending sounds thrilling and intense! It adds a layer of suspense and showcases Kitty Pryde's character growth and determination.”\n\nHonestly, it talks like Carpenter writes.\n\nFirstly, I wanted to see if I could glean a proper villain for this, someone who makes sense but isn’t Sinister (I want someone disposable).\n\nSo I ask it:\n> The most important thing is I need a villain, a plan, and a kind of monster to unleash.\n> \n> The villain needs motivation and the plan needs a purpose (it can't be just \"kill mutants\" if he's unleashing the monsters on the general public).\n> \n> \n> \n> So let's start with the villain, then. What X-Men villains are there, in the history of the X-Men, who might have the means and the motive to unleash an army of monsters on NYC?\n\n\nAnd I get some candidates:\n> Mister Sinister: As you mentioned earlier, Mister Sinister could be a compelling choice. Known for his obsession with genetic manipulation and cloning, Sinister could have created or acquired a group of monstrous creations to serve his sinister purposes. His motives could revolve around conducting experiments, advancing his knowledge of mutant genetics, or seeking revenge against the X-Men.\n> \n> \n> \n> Bastion: Bastion is an advanced Sentinel and a notable X-Men adversary. In the comics, he has the ability to create and control Nimrod-class Sentinels, highly advanced and adaptable robotic beings. Bastion could have a plan to unleash a swarm of these formidable machines on New York City, which could be considered as monstrous threats to both mutants and humans alike.\n> \n> \n> \n> Sugar Man: Sugar Man is a character associated with the Age of Apocalypse storyline, a dark alternate timeline. He is a twisted geneticist known for his experiments and creations. While Sugar Man may not be as well-known to casual viewers, his abilities and motivations could be adapted for your movie, making him a formidable villain who unleashes his monstrous creations on the city.\n> \n> \n> \n> Omega Red: Omega Red is a mutant with a dark past and deadly abilities, including the ability to emit \"death spores\" that drain the life force of his victims. His storyline could involve him seeking revenge against mutants and humans, using his deadly spores to create monstrous creatures or even infect individuals to transform them into mindless monsters.\n\n\nI’m intrigued by “Sugar Man”, who I’ve never heard of.\n![](dayone-moment://61F7A29FBF264DE296C3522154264824)\nSo I run with him.\n> I'm intrigued by some of these ideas. Is Sugar Man the type of character to cut deals with other shady characters and groups, but ultimately betray them?\n\n\nAnd I get a response that includes\n> Yes, Sugar Man is known for his manipulative nature and willingness to make deals with other shady characters and groups. He is often depicted as a conniving and self-serving character, always looking out for his own interests. While Sugar Man may initially collaborate with other villains or organizations to further his goals or gain resources, he has a tendency to betray his allies when it suits him.\n> \n> \n> \n> His primary allegiance is typically to himself, and he is willing to exploit others for his own gain. This makes him a compelling choice for a character who could form alliances, strike deals, and work behind the scenes while secretly plotting his own agenda. His ability to manipulate genetics and conduct experiments could tie into his plan to unleash an army of monsters, using them as tools to achieve his ultimate goals.\n\n\nI ask for options for the monsters the villain needs to create/have, and get some options: \\- Mutant hounds - no. That don’t work.\n\\- Adaptoids - interesting but not\n\\- Sugar Man’s genetically engineered monsters - gene-spliced things that he could make. This ultimately makes the most sense, though it’s a shame not to pull some other corner of the universe in\n\\- Savage Land creatures - interesting\n\\- Symbiotes - hell no\n\\- Brood Hybrids - I mentioned that I’d thought of the Brood but it don’t really work, and it suggest gene-splicing them\n\nI tried to get ChatGPT to come up with a plan for Sugar Man that has him betray the Hellfire Club and then unleash his monsters on NYC, and I got some generic stuff that doesn’t help much. Everyone wants power, everyone shares research and tech. Nothing juicy.\n\nThen I asked for a plot breakdown in 3 acts. Then I offered some notes to get a revised draft. And here we see the limits of the AI, as we lose some of the order and plot I tried to establish.\n![](dayone-moment://BD9551FE755C4460ACFD3D3FE647BEAE)\n![](dayone-moment://467E517B7D8F410B8DC414465CABD247)\n![](dayone-moment://9B9B8A4C507B43809148F62F5C0CAF0A)\n![](dayone-moment://86CDBF5260694A56B315E19725DB87A5)\n![](dayone-moment://9D6951932268405C910E3C86BA8DB8B6)\n\nSo this ain’t ready for prime time. I had wanted it to structure out the plot I described, and it can’t do it. It also can’t give me any specific and interesting plot or character nuances - just generic arcs and platitudes.\n\nIf I were to take actual steps after this, it would probably be to:\n- Just go with Sinister (or a Sinister clone)\n- Throw out the breakdown the AI came up with and write my own.\n* * *\n\nJust shot Miguel a message in the group chat, since he seems to know his way around this stuff, on a technical level.\n> @Michael Carpenter\n> \n> You're our resident AI expert, and I'm curious what you think about the following:\n> \n> \n> \n> This evening, I opened up the free online instance of Chat GPT ([https://chat.openai.com/](<https://chat.openai.com/>)), which I believe is GPT 3.\n> \n> I explained to it, in detail, my pitch for an X-Men movie, asked it for help brainstorming to fill in some holes, and then asked it to write a plot breakdown using a 3-act structure.\n> \n> \n> \n> \\- It spent a lot of time restating what I was saying using more words. \\- It tried to expand on themes and character arcs but ultimately gave a lot of generic suggestions and platitudes. The AI came across more as someone who likes the sound of their own voice than someone with actual storytelling insight.\n> \n> \\- “It's important to strike a balance between their interactions and the central storyline to ensure that both aspects receive proper attention and development\"\n> \n> \\- “Introducing a tragic element to Kitty's backstory and exploring the bigotry and fear surrounding mutants can add depth and emotional resonance to her character\".\n> \n> \\- Like, thanks GPT, I guess. GPT is restating things in smart-sounding ways, but bringing nothing to the table. Just generic stuff.\n> \n> \\- It can't give me any more interesting motivation for villains to work together than \"they all want power and each others' expertise\"\n> \n> \\- And it can't seem to get the story order/structure/content straight in its final plot breakdown\n> \n> \n> \n> Basically, I tell you all this because I'm curious\n> \n> If you've played with or seen GPT 4, or whatever the newer version of this is... do you think it could do better?\n> \n> Do you think it would be able to come up with more interesting twists, themes, character arcs, character motivations given a larger training data set?\n> \n> Would it be able to keep better track of the discussion so that the final breakdown is more representative of it?\n> \n> \n> \n> (I recognize I'm asking you these questions without giving you any of the conversation itself, but I'm hoping that maybe there's a more generalized answer to this - will the advancement of this GPT tech bring more capability to suggest things beyond the generic and flavorless?)\n\n\n(I asked ChatGPT about the fur in Emma Frost’s costumes early on, so now I can’t share this convo with anyone, because I’m a dumbass)\n* * *\n\nOkay so I want to see if I can at least get it to write up a general summary of the overall pitch we discussed, outside of the two drafts it just did, including all details, and in chronological order.\n\n> Putting aside the last two breakdown drafts you wrote, I'd like to try something else.\n> \n> \n> \n> Over the course of this conversation, we've assembled a pitch for a movie, but we haven't talked about it all in chronological order - our conversation has jumped from theme to theme, topic to topic, rather than following the order of events of our film.\n> \n> \n> \n> Could you write up a summarizing essay that describes the overall pitch, including all details discussed, in the proper order of events?\n\n\nOkay it’s going now, but it’s doing a bunch of the Frodoe things as the breakdowns?\n\nUh oh, it got to the end and then... started over?\n\nIt gave it a name though... X-Men: Dawn of Hope\nWhich is donkin hilarious\nAll it’s missing is the word “Rise”\nIt’s like I keep saying - super generic.\n\nOh, and then it failed entirely. Might regenerate the response. Might reword my question.\n\nOH cannoli We can burn Kitty’s house down in act 2!\nIt put that in act 2 and that *might make sense!*\nIt still doesn’t get that I want the “issue 143” Kitty vs. Monster thing at the *very end*, after everything else.\n![](dayone-moment://67F2710E13B84589AD9E0376F1FA0BAB)\n![](dayone-moment://2BA023C6FE774FFFA30C1FF55983E975)\n\nOk let’s try something simpler.\n> Putting aside the last two breakdown drafts you wrote, I'd like to try something else.\n> \n> \n> \n> Could you write an essay summarizing the pitch we've assembled over the course of this conversation , with all the details we discussed?\n\n![](dayone-moment://87D1BDC19D5342E8B570D04A3DEB65B1)\n![](dayone-moment://FD92788E4A06406EBC8E51B84E20847E)\n![](dayone-moment://812CBB6D96994090959B168AE12396C3)\n\nSo it’s still crap BUT\nI’m seeing the value here because it unwittingly came close to an actual good, obvious idea which hasn’t crossed my mind - Kitty’s house is burned down, leaving her in a place where she has to decide who she is going to be, coming out of that tragedy. Because there’s going to be temptation to “go bad”, and maybe that’s the place for Emma. From the beginning, Emma is like “come to my school instead of Xavier’s”, and maybe that temptation strengthens after the tragedy, as Emma tries to capitalize on it (but also, I guess, feels sympathy, because this is a character who will eventually become a hero?)\n\nSo I guess the real thing to do here is flesh out my own 3 act structure.\n\nHmm. Hmm wait\nSo I was trying to figure out...\nOkay\nSo Emma ties directly to Kitty. Emma ties directly to the villain. What ties those two ties together?\nEmma sympathizes with Kitty, wants to get her out of the X-Men before the villain, Sinister, wipes them out.\nI like that. I can work with that.\nShe just underestimates the scope of his plans.\n\nSo I guess I see the value of this? In that it accidentally gives me good ideas.\n\nOkay\nSo *now* maybe the thing to do is work out my own 3 act structure.\nYou know, instead of going to bed.\nAnd also I’m watching this Nando vid about the Superman casting.\n* * *\n\n## Prologue\n- “10 Years Ago...” or whatever. Sometime in the past.\n- We see the original five (O5) X-Men - Cyclops, Jean, Beast, Angel, Iceman - on a mission. We don’t see their faces, just their silhouettes and their powers.\n- Their mission? Free mutants who have been kidnapped and experimented on by Mr. Sinister.\n- Sinister is our POV character for this prologue\n- Sinister knows who they are, and in particular knows who Xavier (and Cyclops) is\n- In the course of this mission, Sinister is “killed”\n- End of prologue\n## Act 1\n- Autumn, present day\n- Kitty Pryde is a kid in Deerfield, Illinois, whose mutant powers manifest in school.\n- She confides in her parents, and her parents are worried for her safety.\n- She is visited by Emma Frost, the headmistress of the Massachusetts Academy. She wears a sharp white suit.\n- Emma leaves, Xavier enters, accompanied by, I dunno, let’s say Ororo Monroe and Scott Summers.\n- So, Kitty packs her bags, says goodbye to Dorian and Regis, and leaves for Xavier’s\n- Let’s close out this act with a full villain intro\n## Act 2\n- Kitty is settling into the school routine, going to classes, getting to know her new friends\n- Kitty, Ororo, and Peter go out in public - to the mall or something.\n- We’ll squeeze in another Emma/Sinister interlude somewhere in this act.\n- Winter break happens - most of the students go home for the season.\n- Back at the mansion, Kurt tells his story - hunted like an animal in his native Germany.\n- Kitty ends up running off, finds herself in a cafe or something.\n- Kitty returns to the mansion. Perhaps she asks her friends about Xavier’s dream\n- And then the doorbell rings\n- Scott now must explain the secret history of the school to our four protagonists\n- Scott says they need to re-form the X-Men\n## Act 3\n- It’s almost Christmas\n- Kitty is inspired by the Avengers (etc.) and while Scott and Xavier work out where Sinister is going to attack, she designs costumes for her and her friends\n- Meanwhile, Sinister betrays Emma - thanks for the funding, I’ll be killing you now and your Hellfire Club next\n- Sinister unleashes an army of his DNA beasts on Manhattan - beasts based on all 5 original X-Men, lasering and freezing and telekinesing people left and right\n- The new X-Men, in costume, climb into the Blackbird in the hanger - we see Kitty taking an interest in the controls and everything. She’s smart, and we watch her quietly learn how Cyclops starts the engines.\n- Having cleared the city streets, the X-Men advance of Sinister’s stronghold, wherever that is.\n- Emma escapes, having asserted mental control over the beast. Once it’s blown the door off its hinges, she has it kill itself.\n- So that’s that. Threat ended, civilians safe, few casualties, bad guy dead, the X-Men are heroes.\n- We have us a final gathering at Rockefeller Center, where we resolve character narrative threads\n- And that’s the end!\n- Just kidding.\n## Act 4\n- Surprise! Kinda. If you haven’t read the rest of my pitch before.\n- Everyone goes their separate ways for Christmas Eve\n- Kitty retires to the mansion alone\n- A DNA beast - let’s say it’s a Beast one, to emphasize the physicality and mobility - escaped and snuck into the mansion\n- So we do that whole thing.\n- Our last 20 minutes is this Alien/Aliens-inspired thing.\n- Kitty uses the acrobatics we saw on display in gym class in tandem with the phasing power stuff she figured out in the mall, and ultimately kills the beast with the engine thruster on the Blackbird that she figured out how to operate.\n- And *that’s* the end of the movie.\n\nI think that covers everything, and gives us a fuller pitch.\nOkay it’s 11:50 I need to go to bed.\n\nMy biggest issue with this pitch right now is Sinister, but I’ve practically locked him in with the X-Men DNA beasts. Oh well.\nCould use some Sentinels too. ¯\\\\_(ツ)\\_/¯ \n\nFunny how, by screwing up which act the house burning down belonged in and putting Emma in a sentence nearby some other concept, the AI accidentally gave me key missing pieces/ideas that tied everything together. The burning house as Kitty’s low point in her arc and Emma as the temptation to run away. \n* * *\n\nStray thoughts from following morning:\n\n(Also I feel like it kinda makes sense that an AI like this would plot and analyze story like a high schooler bullcannoliting an essay - it’s just coming up with the “most likely” words for its response, right?)<br>\n\n\n(A text generating AI firmly skewing/weighted towards the average, so that everything is the Frodoe, everything is generic.)\n\nThesis: Robots are still only better than humans at uncreative, repetitive, generic tasks - even if those tasks now include text and image generation.\n\nI'm concerned about Mortimer's ambitions towards copywriting and proofreading, then.\n"